Haakon Studebaker <heptapod@yahoo.com> writes:

> Can anyone suggest a good book on C programming geared towards novices? I'm 
> looking for something to introduce me to programming so I can alter stuff 
> in Nethack.

First, be warned:  As a newcomer to programming, the following 
is most likely:

- Your 'new nethack' will crash.  

- It will behave strangely for strange reasons.  

- You will spend hours finding trivial errors.  

- At the end, you will have minor changes after days 
  or weeks of frustrating work.  

Altering a complex, evolved code is painful at best, 
even for experienced programmers.  The somewhat 
sparse comments do not provide much help in 
understanding what's going on.  Before you alter 
something, you should study the code carefully; 
maybe you can set as a first goal to write a 
definite spoiler first about the things you want 
to change; or find in the code and verify the 
known spoiler's explanations.  You want to do 
this on an OS which has the 'grep' command 
or some relative; the code is dissected into many 
smaller files, and you have to trace function 
calls and the like through the files.  

If you still want to have a go, I liked "Practical C 
Programming"; however, it is maybe best to get[1] some 
book about computer science to learn a bit more about 
some concepts behind.  If you can manage it, Knuth's 
books are the best, of course.  

Best,
Jakob

[1] Better books are usual more expensive; try to get 
access to a library with 'better' CS books.  As a rule 
of thumb, a row of catchy titles like 'How to program 
in java and cook underwater' or 'Python - The answer  
to your prayer' or 'C for completely inferior idiots 
with hygiene problems' is not so good; a row with titles 
from O'Reilly is more promising.  

